NAME
       crontab - edit, list or remove a user's table of repeating jobs

SYNOPSIS
       crontab [-u USER] -e
       crontab [-u USER] -l
       crontab [-u USER] -r [-i]
       crontab [-u USER] FILE

DESCRIPTION
       Every user may have a crontab: a list of commands that the crond
       service runs again and again at set times. crontab -e opens it in
       your editor (vi unless EDITOR or VISUAL say otherwise); when you save
       and quit, it is checked and installed. crontab FILE replaces the whole
       table with FILE (crontab - reads it from standard input).

       Only root may use -u to work on another user's table. Tables are kept
       in /var/spool/cron/USER; do not edit those files directly.

   Lines of a crontab
       Each job is one line of five time fields and a command:

           minute  hour  day-of-month  month  day-of-week  command
           0-59    0-23  1-31          1-12   0-7 (0 and 7 are Sunday)

       In each field:

       *          every value
       5          exactly 5
       1,15       either 1 or 15
       9-17       9 through 17
       */10       every 10th value (0, 10, 20...)
       1-5        Monday to Friday (in day-of-week)

       Month and day names (jan, mon) may be used too. When both day fields
       are restricted, the job runs when either one matches.

       Lines starting with # are comments. A line NAME=value sets a variable
       for the jobs below it (MAILTO=, PATH=, SHELL=). A % in the command
       means a new line, so write \% inside date formats.

       The command runs with /bin/sh, in the user's home directory and with a
       small PATH; give full paths. Anything it prints is mailed to the user,
       so redirect output to a file (or to /dev/null) if you do not want it.

   Shortcuts
       @reboot    once when crond starts (at boot)
       @hourly    0 * * * *
       @daily     0 0 * * *
       @weekly    0 0 * * 0
       @monthly   0 0 1 * *

OPTIONS
       -e     Edit the table (create it when there is none).

       -l     Print the table.

       -r     Remove the table.

       -i     With -r, ask before removing.

       -u USER
              Work on USER's table (root only).

EXAMPLES
       As root, make student's job run every weekday at 14:30:

           crontab -u student -e
           30 14 * * 1-5 /usr/bin/echo "hello" >> /home/student/hello.log

       Install a table from a file without an editor (handy in scripts):

           echo '*/5 * * * * logger "cert lab check"' | crontab -u student -

       Check what is installed:

           crontab -l -u student

       Run a job every Sunday at 03:00 for the system (in /etc/cron.d/backup,
       note the extra user field):

           0 3 * * 0 root /usr/local/bin/backup.sh

FILES
       /var/spool/cron/USER
              The users' tables.

       /etc/crontab, /etc/cron.d/*
              System tables. They have a sixth field, the user to run as,
              before the command.

       /etc/cron.hourly, cron.daily, cron.weekly, cron.monthly
              Scripts run by run-parts (and anacron) at those intervals.

       /etc/cron.allow, /etc/cron.deny
              Who may use crontab. If cron.allow exists, only the users in it
              may; otherwise everyone except those in cron.deny.

NOTES
       crond must be running (it is by default). Check the times in
       /var/log/cron or with journalctl -u crond. For a single run at a later
       time use at; systemd timers are the other way to repeat a job.

SEE ALSO
       crontab(5), cron(8), at(1), systemd.timer(5)
